From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Subject: [PATCH] EDEN_UP_ASYNC_TRANSLATION=1: new native graphics pipelines are translated on VkPipelineTranslator threads, the draw waits up to 2 ms (4 ms per frame) and is then skipped until a later draw adopts the pipeline (Djihads80/Eglantine 0f1ae99); off by default

Port of Djihads80/Eglantine 0f1ae99 (branch fix/oot-map-change, based on Wael3rd/Eglantine-MKW
6d8f95f08e and 9a876a744e): "OoT cold-cache boot and walk: ~3.4 s of GPU-thread pipeline time
before, ~0.9 s after". A new graphics pipeline is translated (lowered code -> IR -> SPIR-V) on the
GPU thread before its draw; at a first visit that is the GPU thread's time, frame by frame. It
helps where pipelines are really new: cold caches, places the prep list misses, mesh pipelines.
It is not expected to help the door hitch in the play env (see Falsify).

With EDEN_UP_ASYNC_TRANSLATION=1 (Common::PeSwitch, exactly "1"):
- CreateGraphicsPipeline() builds the environments on the GPU thread as now and tries the kept
  translation first (EDEN_UP_SHADER_PREP, unchanged: fast, on the GPU thread). Only without one,
  and only when every stage is a lowered native program and the draw may be skipped (depth on or
  more than 6 elements, BuiltPipeline's test), the environments are copied and detached from the
  3D engine (GraphicsEnvironment::Detach) and the translation is queued on VkPipelineTranslator
  threads. The GPU thread waits up to EDEN_UP_ASYNC_TRANSLATION_WAIT_MS (default 2) per pipeline
  and EDEN_UP_ASYNC_TRANSLATION_FRAME_MS (default 4) per frame, and not at all behind a backlog
  (more pending than threads). Ready in time: the pipeline goes on like a kept one (disk cache
  entry, runtime report). Not ready: the draw is skipped; the next slow-path lookup adopts finished
  translations into graphics_cache, marks them used (so the eviction pass, every 600 frames past
  frame 9000, does not take one as unused before its first draw) and writes them to the disk
  cache. Compute pipelines, Switch 1 programs and programs not lowered yet stay on the GPU thread.
- A draw that can't be skipped never waits behind the queue: if its key is queued and no worker
  has started the job, the GPU thread takes it back (a claim flag per job, set once by whoever
  translates it) and translates it as without the switch; the worker that reaches the job drops
  it. Only a job a worker has already started is waited for whole (HangSite "waiting for a
  pipeline translation").
- Cap: at most 4 jobs per thread pending, and at most EDEN_UP_ASYNC_TRANSLATION_PENDING_MIB
  (default 16, 1 to 1024) of copied constant buffers held by queued jobs (checked before a job's
  copies are made, so at most one job, under 7 MiB, goes past it). Over the cap a new pipeline is
  not copied or queued and its draw is skipped; its key is remembered, so its later draws are
  skipped without building environments again until there is room, when it is made from the
  start (or at once on the GPU thread for a draw that can't be skipped).
- Out of memory: everything queueing needs (job node, environment copies, constant buffer
  copies, the pending entry, the queued task) is made in one block before the job is queued. A
  std::bad_alloc there leaves nothing queued or pending, and the pipeline is translated on the
  GPU thread as without the switch, under its existing guard ("dropped" log, the session goes
  on); EDEN_NX2_BAD_ALLOC_ENDS=1 still ends the session. The disk cache entry is written from the
  job's own node, moved to the writer whole (no copy of the environments on the GPU thread); a
  writer queue without memory leaves that pipeline out of the disk cache, as the writer does.
- Threads: EDEN_UP_ASYNC_TRANSLATION_THREADS=<n> (1 to the builder count), default half the
  builders, at least 1, at most 4 (upstream: half, no cap; each translation holds its shader pools
  and copied constant buffers, and each thread its own heap arena). Background placement, i.e. the
  builders' low priority; the take-back above keeps that off the GPU thread's critical path.
- Not tied to the asynchronous shaders setting (upstream requires it; off on the PS5, rejected in
  t77-s). Only translation is skipped over: once adopted, a pipeline whose driver build is not done
  is waited for as before, by the scheduler worker in ConfigureDraw, not the GPU thread.

Detach (shader_environment.*): enabled constant buffers are copied whole with ReadWordsUnsafe
(reads as Read<u32> does: no flush, Switch 2 syncpoint shim included) at the moment the GPU thread
would have read them; a buffer claiming more than 64 KiB is not detached (the copy could not answer
reads past it). The texture pool is located, not copied: its descriptors are read on the worker
through the same ReadTextureInfo with ReadBlockUnsafe (EDEN_NX2_TIC_NULL_PAST_POOL and the past-pool
read behave as on the GPU thread; upstream reads by device address and zeroes past the pool). A pool
the GPU wrote (IsMemoryDirty) or with a limit over 2^20 is not detached: translated on the GPU
thread as before. The viewport flag and the HLE replacement table are copied. The copy is a
shared_ptr, so copies of the environment don't copy the buffers, and it is freed on the worker
right after translation. On the worker, storage pointer checks still read the GPU page table (and,
for indirect ones, the guest word) through gpu_memory, as upstream; the native geometry check asks
the environment (thread_local translating_detached), not the engine.

Interplay checked:
- EDEN_UP_SHADER_PREP: kept translations are tried first and are not changed; PrepareKept runs at
  boot before any draw. Async only covers keys the kept store lacks or refuses (prep_rejected), the
  places the prep list misses. Translations written to vulkan.bin are kept at the next boot as now.
- 0494 (UB-056, EDEN_UP_FAST_CFG claim-once builds): the translator constructs the pipeline with
  worker_thread = the builder workers, so the driver build (RADV's NIR compiler) is queued and
  claimed on VkPipelineBuilder threads exactly as from the GPU thread; ConfigureDraw's BuildFirst
  moves it to the front once the pipeline is adopted. vk_graphics_pipeline.* is not touched. The
  job claim here is separate: it only decides who translates.
- Heap wait (EDEN_UP_HEAP_WAIT_MS, headless/heap_arenas.inc): the translators are named
  "VkPipelineTranslator" (heap record "VkPipelineTrans", prefix "VkPipeline"), so they may wait for
  memory like the builders. Their RADV calls are only the checked object creations of the pipeline
  constructor (shader modules, set and pipeline layouts, update templates, a new descriptor bank),
  never the compiler. The GPU thread waits on them for at most the 2/4 ms budget, except a draw
  that can't be skipped whose job a worker has already started: a translator waiting for memory
  can hold that one for up to HEAP_WAIT_MS (the GPU thread's texture collector cannot help it
  meanwhile). A worker never allocates after translating: its job node is made on the GPU thread
  and handed back with a list splice.
- Worker translation is CreateGraphicsPipeline(pools, ...), which boot already runs on several
  builder threads at once; its shared state (descriptor banks, attributeless set, mesh allow list,
  shader notify, Churn) is locked or atomic. The rasterizer looks the pipeline up before it takes
  the buffer/texture cache locks, so no wait holds them.
- 0499 (EDEN_UP_MESH_CACHE): a pipeline translated in time takes CreateGraphicsPipeline()'s own
  tail, so its mesh report follows 0499's rule; only the disk write uses the job's copies.
- 0501 canonical keys: graphics_key is used as the slow path has it. Ahead guard: environment
  reads are not guarded in either path, and Detach reads when the GPU thread would.
- tools/prepare-vulkan-port.py: every anchor and expression count it checks in vk_pipeline_cache.cpp
  is unchanged (slow-path tail with is_new, CreateGraphicsPipeline() opening, main_pools 2,
  TranslateProgram 3, EmitSPIRV 1+1, BuildShader 2, CFG 2+1, constructor end, destructor boundary),
  on T88 and on T88 + the series below.

Measuring: "Graphics pipeline built on the GPU thread in N ms" lines (the card's First-visit stutter
section) keep reporting each new pipeline's GPU-thread time; with the switch that is environments +
kept check + detach + queue + wait, also for one left translating or put off. New line every 10 s
with activity: "EDEN_UP_ASYNC_TRANSLATION in the last 10 s: Q queued (K KiB copied, P ms preparing,
at most N pending holding B KiB), R ready in time, S draws skipped, D not detached, C over the cap,
M without memory; W ms waited, H whole waits (... ms), T taken back; A adopted (F failed), ... ms
on the workers". Skipped draws also show in the rasterizer's first 64 "Draw skipped (no pipeline)"
warnings. EDEN_PERF_COST shader_* costs now include worker time.

Falsify: not on door-walk slots. The t94-a card (play env, prep and canonical keys) shows 0 new
pipelines and 0-154 ms of GPU-thread pipeline time per run, worst frames 632-655 ms and ~3.8 s of
texture decodes per run: translation is not the door hitch there. Test it on the cold arm
(EDEN_UP_PIPELINE_COLD=1 with prep off): GPU-thread pipeline ms and worst frames with and without
the switch, flashes per run (a skipped draw can show as a missing object for a few frames), and
the 10 s line's over the cap, taken back and peak pending KiB. If peak pending sits at the cap
for long, retry with EDEN_UP_ASYNC_TRANSLATION_PENDING_MIB=32; if memory is tight, with
EDEN_UP_ASYNC_TRANSLATION_THREADS=2. Places the prep list misses and mesh pipelines (one mesh
build took 65.9 ms in run783) are the other cases it can help.

Unset or anything but "1": translation_workers stays null; the slow path, CreateGraphicsPipeline(),
the destructor and the geometry check take the old branches (translation_reported stays false, so
ReportNewPipelineKey runs as before; the tail's job list is empty, so the disk cache entry is
written as before); GraphicsEnvironment's detached flag is never set, so every read goes to the
engine as before; ReadTextureInfo flushes as before (its new parameter defaults to false). No cache
key, file format or serialization change.

Not ported: upstream's TakeBackgroundPipeline path (ours has no background pipelines) and its
ShaderCache::device_memory move (descriptors are read by GPU address here).

Checked: git apply --check clean on the T88 core, and on T88 + 0493, 0494, 0495,
0498, 0499, 0500, 0501, 0503 (0496, 0497 and 0502 need files the T88 core copy lacks and touch
none of these files); applied before or after those, both orders give the same tree. Not
compiled, not run.

Rebased onto T98 (0505-0507 and, for the later two, the drafts before it) with patch -F3; no hunk rejected.

